Longer version:

Trust carries two lineages, and they are tracked separately.

Files inherited from rust-lang/rust (the compiler crates, the standard library,
and the bootstrap, documentation and test infrastructure) remain the copyright
of the Rust project's contributors. Trust's edits inside those files are
contributions to that work, offered under the same terms.

Files Trust wrote are Copyright (c) 2026 Andrew Yates: the verification crates
under `crates/`, `targo-trust/`, the verification pass and the codegen backend
that live inside the compiler tree, the Trust-only toolchain front ends, and
this project's own documentation, scripts and tooling.

`REUSE.toml` records which lineage each path belongs to. Neither claim displaces
the other, and both are offered under the same two licenses, so a downstream
user needs only those two.

Copyrights in Trust are retained by their contributors. No copyright assignment
is required to contribute.

Some files include explicit copyright notices and/or license notices. For full
authorship information, see the version control history; for the inherited
files, see also <https://thanks.rust-lang.org>.

Except as otherwise noted, Trust is licensed under the Apache License, Version
2.0 <LICENSE-APACHE> or <http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0> or the MIT
license <LICENSE-MIT> or <http://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>, at your option.

We track licenses for third-party materials in two ways:

* We use [REUSE](https://reuse.software) to track license information for
  in-tree source files - those authored by Trust, those inherited from
  rust-lang/rust, and those authored by third parties alike. See `REUSE.toml`,
  and our cached output of the `reuse` tool which is committed to
  `license-metadata.json`.
* We use `cargo` to track license information for out-of-tree dependencies.

These two sources of information are collected by the tool `generate-copyright`
into a file called `COPYRIGHT.html`, which is shipped with each binary release
of Trust. Please refer to that file for detailed information as to the
components of any given Trust release. We also produce a `COPYRIGHT-library.html`
file which only covers the subset of source code used in the standard library,
as opposed to the toolchain as a whole.
